--TEST--
`push` refuses a job that can never be attempted, and it does so before it reaches the database — so the refusal does not depend on the server being up — ADR 0084 §§ 1 and 6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6 makes attempts finite, not optional: a job with no attempts is one the
// enqueue that created it has already dead-lettered, which is a call site
// error and not a job. `[queue] max_attempts` cannot say it either — boot
// refuses a `0` there as `E0617` — so this is the same bound one layer up,
// where the number is the program's rather than the operator's.
//
// The block below names a host nothing is listening on. That is the assertion
// this case is really making: if `push` reached the connection before it
// judged its own arguments, this would be an `IOError` about a refused socket
// rather than the `LogicError` about the argument, and it would say something
// different on a machine that happened to have a server there.
try {
    var $id = Core\Queue::push("jobs/send-receipt.nvs", {maxAttempts: 0});
    echo "enqueued", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

echo "still running", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A queue whose connection resolves at boot -- `connection` names a block that
# exists, which is all `nvs_config::queue` checks -- and whose port has nothing
# behind it. Reaching it would be the failure this case is written to rule out.
[queue]
connection = "main"

[db.main]
driver = "postgres"
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 1
--EXPECT--
Core\Queue::push: `maxAttempts` of 0 asks for a job that is dead-lettered by the enqueue that created it — ADR 0084 § 6 makes attempts finite, not optional
still running
